==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[attonce]] {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{term|at}} + {{term|once}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ət ˈwʌns/}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|head=[[at]] [[once]]|-}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} In [[one]] group; [[together]].
#* '''1590''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''{{w|The Faerie Queene}}'', III.4:
#*: Those two great champions did '''attonce''' pursew / The fearefull damzell with incessany payns [...].
# {{context|idiomatic|lang=en}} [[at the same time|At the same time]]; [[simultaneously]].
#: {{usex|lang=en|Can you pat your head and rub your belly '''at once'''?|lang=en}}
#: {{usex|lang=en|He tried to eat four cookies '''at once.'''|lang=en}}
# {{context|idiomatic|lang=en}} [[immediately|Immediately]]; [[now]]; [[right away]].
#*{{quote-book|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}}|chapter=3
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp]
|passage=One saint's day in mid-term a certain newly appointed suffragan-bishop came to the school chapel, and there preached on “The Inner Life.”&nbsp; He '''at once''' secured attention by his informal method, and when presently the coughing of Jarvis […] interrupted the sermon, he altogether captivated his audience with a remark about cough lozenges being cheap and easily procurable.}}
#*{{quote-book|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=19|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=As soon as Julia returned with a constable, Timothy, who was on the point of exhaustion, prepared to give over to him gratefully. The newcomer turned out to be a powerful youngster, fully trained and eager to help, and he stripped off his tunic '''at once'''.}}
#: {{usex|lang=en|Tell the doctor to come '''at once'''. She is having a baby.|lang=en}}

